NDO" TTY="SYN" type="syn">Loeys-Dietz syndrome 3</Name><Name SDUI="MONDO:0013426" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="AN1638929" SAB="MONDO" TTY="SYN" type="syn">Loeys-Dietz syndrome type 3</Name><Name SDUI="MONDO:0013426" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="AN1651458" SAB="MONDO" TTY="SYN" type="syn">Loeys-Dietz syndrome, type 1C (formerly)</Name><Name SDUI="MONDO:0013426" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="AN1651460" SAB="MONDO" TTY="SYN" type="syn">Loeys-Dietz syndrome, type 1C, formerly</Name><Name SDUI="MONDO:0013426" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="MONDO_0013426" SAB="MONDO" TTY="SYN" type="syn">aneurysm-osteoarthritis syndrome</Name><Name SDUI="Orphanet_284984" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="AN1761367" SAB="ORDO" TTY="PT" type="preferred">Aneurysm-osteoarthritis syndrome</Name><Name SDUI="NBK1133" SCUI="GTRT000016102" CODE="NBK1133" SAB="GENEREVIEWS" TTY="PT" type="syn">SMAD3-Related Loeys-Dietz Syndrome</Name></Names><Definitions><Definition source="GeneReviews">Loeys-Dietz syndrome (LDS) is characterized by vascular findings (cerebral, thoracic, and abdominal arterial aneurysms and/or dissections), skeletal manifestations (pectus excavatum or pectus carinatum, scoliosis, joint laxity, arachnodactyly, talipes equinovarus, and cervical spine malformation and/or instability), craniofacial features (hypertelorism, strabismus, bifid uvulaÂ / cleft palate, and craniosynostosis that can involve any sutures), and cutaneous findings (velvety and translucent skin, easy bruising, and dystrophic scars). Individuals with LDS are predisposed to widespread and aggressive arterial aneurysms and pregnancy-related complications including uterine rupture and death. Individuals with LDS can show a strong predisposition for allergic/inflammatory disease including asthma, eczema, and reactions to food or environmental allergens. There is also an increased incidence of gastrointestinal inflammation including eosinophilic esophagitis and gastritis or inflammatory bowel disease. Wide variation in the distribution and severity of clinical features can be seen in individuals with LDS, even among affected individuals within a family who have the same pathogenic variant.</Definition></Definitions><Chromosome>15</Chromosome><Cytogenetic>15q22.33</Cytogenetic><ModesOfInheritance><ModeOfInheritance uid="141047" CUI="C0443147" TUI="T170"><Name>Autosomal dominant inheritance</Name><SemanticType>Intellectual Product</SemanticType><SAB>ORDO</SAB></ModeOfInheritance></ModesOfInheritance><PharmacologicResponse></PharmacologicResponse><OMIM><MIM>613795</MIM></OMIM><ClinicalFeatures><ClinicalFeature uid="362" CUI="C0003486" TUI="T047" SDUI="HP:0004942"><Name>Aortic aneurysm</Name><SemanticType>Disease or Syndrome</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature><ClinicalFeature uid="8153" CUI="C0003504" TUI="T047" SDUI="HP:0001659"><Name>Aortic regurgitation</Name><SemanticType>Disease or Syndrome</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature><ClinicalFeature uid="2047" CUI="C0003706" TUI="T019" SDUI="HP:0001166"><Name>Arachnodactyly</Name><SemanticType>Congenital Abnormality</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature><ClinicalFeature uid="445" CUI="C0004238" TUI="T033" SDUI="HP:0005110"><Name>Atrial fibrillation</Name><SemanticType>Finding</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature><ClinicalFeature uid="3130" CUI="C0009081" TUI="T019" SDUI="HP:0001762"><Name>Clubfoot</Name><SemanticType>Congenital Abnormality</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature><ClinicalFeature uid="1163" CUI="C0010278" TUI="T047" SDUI="HP:0001363"><Name>Craniosynostosis syndrome</Name><SemanticType>Disease or Syndrome</SemanticType></ClinicalFeature></ClinicalFeatures><RelatedDisorders></RelatedDisorders><SNOMEDCT></SNOMEDCT><AssociatedGenes><Gene gene_id="4088" chromosome="15" cytogen_loc="15q22.33">SMAD3</Gene></AssociatedGenes><ORDO><HierarchyUrl>http://www.orpha.net/consor/cgi-bin/Disease_Classif.php?lng=EN&amp;data_id=156&amp;PatId=20631&amp;search=Disease_Classif_Simple&amp;new=1</HierarchyUrl></ORDO><SemanticTypes><SemanticType TUI="T047">Disease or Syndrome</SemanticType